<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best base layer for skiing is a snug merino wool or synthetic top and bottom that wicks sweat away from your skin. Merino stays warm when damp and resists odor; synthetic dries faster and usually costs less. Choose lightweight for spring and active days and midweight or heavier for cold days, and never wear cotton.</p></div>

<h2>What a base layer does</h2>
<p>Your base layer sits against your skin. Its main job is moving sweat away from you so you stay dry, because damp skin gets cold fast on a chairlift. Warmth comes mostly from your mid layer and shell; the base layer keeps the whole system working. Read <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %> for how the layers fit together.</p>

<h2>Merino wool vs synthetic</h2>
<h3>Merino wool</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Soft, fine wool that isn't itchy like old-school wool.</li>
  <li>Stays warm even when it's damp.</li>
  <li>Resists odor, so you can wear it more than one day on a trip.</li>
  <li>Dries slower than synthetic and usually costs more.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Synthetic (polyester and blends)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Dries quickly and wicks well during hard effort.</li>
  <li>Durable and usually less expensive.</li>
  <li>Holds odor more than merino.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Blends</h3>
<p>Merino-synthetic blends try to combine merino's warmth and odor resistance with synthetic durability and faster drying. They're a solid middle ground.</p>
<p>Shop <%= gear_link "merino base layers", "merino base layer" %> or <%= gear_link "synthetic base layers", "synthetic base layer" %> depending on your priorities.</p>

<h2>Choose a weight for the conditions</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lightweight:</strong> spring skiing, warm days, or if you run hot. Also good for high-effort days like hiking for turns.</li>
  <li><strong>Midweight:</strong> the most versatile choice for typical winter days.</li>
  <li><strong>Heavyweight:</strong> very cold days, early-morning lifts, or if you run cold.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you can only buy one set, midweight covers the most days. You can add or remove a mid layer to adjust from there.</p>

<h2>Fit</h2>
<p>A base layer should be snug against your skin without restricting movement. Loose fabric can't wick sweat well. Look for flat seams that won't rub under a pack or harness, and tops long enough to stay tucked in when you bend over.</p>
<p>For bottoms, choose full-length leggings that end above your boot cuff or just inside it without bunching. Only your socks should be inside your ski boots; bulky fabric there causes pressure points and cold feet.</p>

<h2>Useful features</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Quarter-zip tops:</strong> let you vent heat on warm days or after a hike.</li>
  <li><strong>Thumb loops:</strong> keep sleeves in place under gloves.</li>
  <li><strong>Crew vs high neck:</strong> a higher collar adds warmth, but a gaiter can do the same job.</li>
  <li><strong>Three-quarter-length bottoms:</strong> stop above the boot so nothing bunches in your boots.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What not to wear</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cotton:</strong> t-shirts, cotton leggings, and cotton underwear absorb sweat and stay wet, which makes you cold.</li>
  <li><strong>Very loose layers:</strong> they trap air badly and don't wick.</li>
  <li><strong>Too many base layers:</strong> two thin base layers usually work worse than one base layer plus a proper mid layer.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Care</h2>
<p>Wash merino on a gentle cycle in cool water and lay it flat or hang it to dry; high heat can shrink it. Synthetic layers can usually go in a normal wash. Skip fabric softener, which can reduce wicking.</p>

<h2>Build the rest of your kit</h2>
<p>Pair your base layer with a fleece or light puffy mid layer and a waterproof shell. Add one pair of thin <%= gear_link "ski socks", "ski socks" %>. Our <%= link_to "ski trip packing list", guide_path("ski-trip-packing-list") %> covers everything else, and the <%= link_to "7-day snow forecast", where_is_it_snowing_path %> tells you how cold and snowy it's likely to be.</p>
